#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166542 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b69474-34f6-5539-accf-5ebfc4f6eb0a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "40513c35-cdde-57b5-be6e-0e19facd138e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8393" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{2x^2+1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2+1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arsinh u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n2x^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\,x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute\n\n$$\nu =\\sqrt{2}\\,x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{π}{\\sqrt{2x^{2} + 1}}dx &= ∫(π)/(\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2}x)^{2} + 1})dx \\\\\n&= π∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}du) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{\\sqrt{2}}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{\\sqrt{2}}arsinh u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{\\sqrt{2}}arsinh (\\sqrt{2}x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25d92a33-95e8-5860-b461-8291dac502d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "34f929f2-63c4-5e9b-bd63-c42839a78ea6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8394" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}} \\,\\textrm{d}x =$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 4}}dx &= \\frac{3}{2}∫(1)/(\\sqrt{(\\frac{x}{2})^{2} - 1})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}} \\cdot 2du \\\\\n&= 3∫\\frac{du}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}} \\\\\n&= 3arcosh u + C \\\\\n&= 3arcosh (\\frac{x}{2}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5aabe1c9-7a37-5b34-893f-9338f5c7c69a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "36f704ba-2263-5351-9a1e-b792b38481bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8395" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{10}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x =$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u =\\artanh u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{10}{1 - 4x^{2}}dx &= 10∫(1)/(1 - (2x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 10∫\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= 5∫\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= 5artanh u + C \\\\\n&= 5artanh 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition\n\n$$\n\\left|2x \\right| < 1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166545 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd13fafc-d765-5e44-aa5a-79c2fff71212" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "b2d41cac-2c65-5d2d-b847-0c1f9bfdf48a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8396" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh{u}+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac {x}{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & 4 & 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ u & 2 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}$\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\\\[5pt] &= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\arcosh u \\Big \\mid _{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\\\[5pt] &= \\arcosh{\\sqrt 5} - \\arcosh{2} \\\\[5pt] &= \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}} \\right) \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167295 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25d92a33-95e8-5860-b461-8291dac502d6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution With Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8955860c-f887-5ddc-a4a3-8870c1bc8876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}arcosh 4x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165319} {:answer/id #uuid "94a1d4c4-9f18-5fe1-83d8-5a979d33cf33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4arsinh (\\frac{x}{4}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165320} {:answer/id #uuid "f444918c-fc22-56fa-bc1d-5c1b47785762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}arcosh 4x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165321} {:answer/id #uuid "00ca2923-b37e-562c-b69c-8193df0ab930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}arsinh 4x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165322} {:answer/id #uuid "36049cc1-15a6-53d0-b3eb-36ecbd586c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16arcosh (\\frac{x}{4}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8955860c-f887-5ddc-a4a3-8870c1bc8876" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}arcosh 4x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165319} :answer-field/id #uuid "d560824a-dc7c-5c34-b0c8-faa143ebdd7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165318}] :question/id #uuid "e1722041-c374-58c7-a9f0-5a7400016292" :question/math-academy-id "q-95432" :question/problem "What is $∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16x^{2} - 1}}dx$?"}]}
